<stack-static>
    <statement><p><p>Wanjiru tosses a soda bottle top <m>{250}</m> times. It lands printed side up <m>{86}</m> times.</p><p>Give each probability exactly, as a fraction or an exact decimal.</p><p>(a) Find the relative frequency with which the bottle top lands printed side up.</p><div><fillin characters="8" name="ans1"/>  </div><p>(b) Estimate the probability that the bottle top does not land printed side up.</p><div><fillin characters="8" name="ans2"/>  </div><p>(c) Estimate how many times the bottle top would land printed side up in <m>{500}</m> tosses.</p><div><fillin characters="6" name="ans3"/> times  </div></p></statement>
    <solution><p><p>(a) Relative frequency <m>= \dfrac{\text{number of times it happened}}{\text{number of trials}} = \dfrac{{86}}{{250}} = {0.344}</m>. This is an estimate of the probability that the bottle top lands printed side up.</p><p>(b) It did not happen in the other <m>{164}</m> of the <m>{250}</m> tosses, so the estimate is <m>1 - {0.344} = {0.656}</m>.</p><p>(c) Expected number <m>=</m> number of trials <m>\times</m> probability <m>= {500} \times {0.344} = {172}</m>. It is an estimate, not a certainty: the real number will usually be close to this, not exactly equal.</p></p></solution>
    <answer><p><m>\frac{43}{125}</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>\frac{82}{125}</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>172</m></p></answer>
</stack-static>